1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,567
A doctor checks your temperature.

2
00:00:02,567 --> 00:00:04,242
A shopkeeper weighs rice.

3
00:00:04,242 --> 00:00:05,676
You measure a table.

4
00:00:06,076 --> 00:00:08,009
Three very different jobs.

5
00:00:08,009 --> 00:00:10,851
But all three use the same basic idea.

6
00:00:11,301 --> 00:00:13,476
Even Diya's recipe card needs it.

7
00:00:13,476 --> 00:00:15,140
It says: sugar, two.

8
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:16,455
Two spoons?

9
00:00:16,455 --> 00:00:17,355
Two cups?

10
00:00:17,314 --> 00:00:18,435
Two kilograms?

11
00:00:18,435 --> 00:00:20,636
The number alone means nothing.

12
00:00:21,136 --> 00:00:23,935
By the end of this
video, you will see why.

13
00:00:23,935 --> 00:00:26,219
It is the first big idea in physics.

14
00:00:26,869 --> 00:00:28,951
So what is a physical quantity?

15
00:00:29,301 --> 00:00:32,028
It is anything in nature
that we can measure.

16
00:00:32,028 --> 00:00:34,630
Length, mass, time, temperature, speed.

17
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:38,679
Kindness and beauty matter, but
no instrument can measure them.

18
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:40,853
So they are not physical quantities.

19
00:00:41,453 --> 00:00:43,490
Every measurement has two parts.

20
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:45,574
A number, called the magnitude.

21
00:00:45,574 --> 00:00:46,502
And a unit.

22
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:51,824
A unit is a fixed amount we all agree
on, like one metre or one kilogram.

23
00:00:52,474 --> 00:00:55,268
Let's measure one pencil
on our measuring bench.

24
00:00:55,618 --> 00:00:58,468
With a centimetre scale, it reads fifteen.

25
00:00:58,468 --> 00:00:59,828
Fifteen centimetres.

26
00:01:00,228 --> 00:01:01,645
Switch to millimetres.

27
00:01:01,645 --> 00:01:03,887
Now it reads one hundred and fifty.

28
00:01:04,287 --> 00:01:05,570
Switch to metres.

29
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:08,054
Now it is only zero point one five.

30
00:01:08,454 --> 00:01:10,001
The pencil never changed.

31
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:12,420
The number and the unit changed together.

32
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,217
Look at the graph.

33
00:01:14,217 --> 00:01:17,230
Unit ten times bigger,
number ten times smaller.

34
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,705
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

35
00:01:21,055 --> 00:01:22,016
Step one.

36
00:01:22,016 --> 00:01:26,189
A quantity Q equals its
number n, times its unit u.

37
00:01:26,189 --> 00:01:27,525
Q equals n u.

38
00:01:28,275 --> 00:01:29,175
Step two.

39
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:33,165
The same quantity in two
different units does not change.

40
00:01:33,165 --> 00:01:35,923
So n one u one equals n two u two.

41
00:01:36,673 --> 00:01:37,589
Step three.

42
00:01:37,589 --> 00:01:41,439
n times u is fixed, so n is
inversely proportional to u.

43
00:01:41,439 --> 00:01:43,946
A bigger unit means a smaller number.

44
00:01:44,696 --> 00:01:45,720
Remember this.

45
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:50,352
A physical quantity is always a number
with a unit, never a number alone.

46
00:01:51,052 --> 00:01:54,274
Now, physicists sort
quantities in two big ways.

47
00:01:54,624 --> 00:01:57,942
The first way: base quantities
and derived quantities.

48
00:01:58,292 --> 00:02:00,653
Base quantities are the building blocks.

49
00:02:00,653 --> 00:02:02,797
The SI system picks seven of them.

50
00:02:03,147 --> 00:02:07,628
Length, mass, time, electric current,
temperature, amount of substance,

51
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:09,121
and luminous intensity.

52
00:02:09,521 --> 00:02:12,218
Derived quantities are built from these.

53
00:02:12,218 --> 00:02:13,168
Take speed.

54
00:02:13,518 --> 00:02:16,365
Speed equals distance divided by time.

55
00:02:16,765 --> 00:02:19,889
Distance is a length, so
it is a base quantity.

56
00:02:19,889 --> 00:02:21,840
Time is also a base quantity.

57
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,980
So speed is made from two base quantities.

58
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:26,975
That is why speed is derived.

59
00:02:27,375 --> 00:02:28,429
Side by side.

60
00:02:28,429 --> 00:02:29,582
Length is base.

61
00:02:29,582 --> 00:02:31,847
You read it straight off a ruler.

62
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:33,402
Speed is derived.

63
00:02:33,402 --> 00:02:36,569
You need a length and a
time, and then you divide.

64
00:02:37,169 --> 00:02:38,208
Now you try.

65
00:02:38,208 --> 00:02:41,678
Is temperature a base quantity
or a derived quantity?

66
00:02:41,678 --> 00:02:43,595
Pause the video and decide.

67
00:02:46,795 --> 00:02:47,695
Base!

68
00:02:47,326 --> 00:02:49,423
Temperature is one of the seven.

69
00:02:49,423 --> 00:02:53,722
A thermometer reads it directly, and
it is not built from length, mass

70
00:02:53,722 --> 00:02:54,622
or time.

71
00:02:55,102 --> 00:02:57,419
The second way: scalars and vectors.

72
00:02:57,769 --> 00:02:59,811
A scalar has only a size.

73
00:02:59,811 --> 00:03:03,138
Five kilograms of rice
is just five kilograms.

74
00:03:03,138 --> 00:03:04,708
No direction needed.

75
00:03:05,058 --> 00:03:07,573
A vector has a size and a direction.

76
00:03:07,573 --> 00:03:10,516
Displacement, velocity
and force are vectors.

77
00:03:10,916 --> 00:03:13,416
Push a box with ten newtons to the right.

78
00:03:13,416 --> 00:03:14,455
It moves right.

79
00:03:14,805 --> 00:03:18,805
Push with the same ten newtons
to the left, and it moves left.

80
00:03:18,805 --> 00:03:20,686
Same size, different result.

81
00:03:20,686 --> 00:03:22,482
So force needs a direction.

82
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:24,067
Your turn.

83
00:03:24,067 --> 00:03:26,561
Is velocity a scalar or a vector?

84
00:03:26,561 --> 00:03:27,937
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:31,137 --> 00:03:32,037
A vector!

86
00:03:31,985 --> 00:03:34,694
Velocity is speed in a given direction.

87
00:03:34,694 --> 00:03:38,954
Fifty kilometres per hour north
is not the same as fifty south.

88
00:03:39,604 --> 00:03:41,874
Let's sort six quantities on the board.

89
00:03:42,224 --> 00:03:44,544
Mass and time are base, and scalar.

90
00:03:44,894 --> 00:03:47,599
Speed and density are derived, and scalar.

91
00:03:47,949 --> 00:03:51,336
Velocity and force are
derived, and they are vectors.

92
00:03:51,686 --> 00:03:53,589
Notice one box stays empty.

93
00:03:53,589 --> 00:03:56,684
None of the seven base
quantities is a vector.

94
00:03:57,334 --> 00:03:59,070
Now, one worked example.

95
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:02,274
A steel rod is two point five metres long.

96
00:04:02,274 --> 00:04:04,358
How long is it in centimetres?

97
00:04:04,858 --> 00:04:05,987
Use the rule.

98
00:04:05,987 --> 00:04:09,665
n two equals n one times
u one, divided by u two.

99
00:04:10,415 --> 00:04:12,440
Here n one is two point five.

100
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:13,872
u one is one metre.

101
00:04:13,872 --> 00:04:15,544
u two is one centimetre.

102
00:04:16,294 --> 00:04:19,050
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

103
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:22,525
So n two is two point
five times one hundred.

104
00:04:23,275 --> 00:04:25,225
That is two hundred and fifty.

105
00:04:25,225 --> 00:04:28,267
The rod is two hundred and
fifty centimetres long.

106
00:04:28,917 --> 00:04:30,380
Now, the exam trap.

107
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:35,654
Many students say electric current is a
vector, because it flows in a direction.

108
00:04:36,004 --> 00:04:37,699
But current is a scalar.

109
00:04:37,699 --> 00:04:42,028
At a junction, two amperes and three
amperes always make five amperes,

110
00:04:42,028 --> 00:04:43,365
whatever the angle.

111
00:04:43,365 --> 00:04:45,360
Vectors do not add like that.

112
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:47,374
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:47,374 --> 00:04:51,702
As quick one mark questions: spot the
base quantity, the derived one, or

114
00:04:51,702 --> 00:04:52,602
the vector.

115
00:04:53,144 --> 00:04:54,427
Exam level now.

116
00:04:54,427 --> 00:04:58,612
Which set has only derived
quantities that are also scalars?

117
00:04:58,612 --> 00:04:59,950
Pause and solve.

118
00:05:05,350 --> 00:05:06,694
The answer is A.

119
00:05:06,694 --> 00:05:08,559
Speed, density and work.

120
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:11,447
B has mass and time, both base.

121
00:05:11,447 --> 00:05:13,248
C has force, a vector.

122
00:05:13,748 --> 00:05:16,532
D has current and temperature, both base.

123
00:05:16,532 --> 00:05:19,276
Only A is fully derived and fully scalar.

124
00:05:19,876 --> 00:05:21,337
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:21,337 --> 00:05:22,838
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:26,038 --> 00:05:28,875
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:29,275 --> 00:05:30,175
One.

128
00:05:29,744 --> 00:05:32,715
Why is sugar, two, not
a complete measurement?

129
00:05:33,615 --> 00:05:34,515
Two.

130
00:05:34,129 --> 00:05:36,356
Why is speed a derived quantity?

131
00:05:37,256 --> 00:05:38,156
Three.

132
00:05:37,854 --> 00:05:41,236
Is electric current a
scalar or a vector, and why?

133
00:05:42,336 --> 00:05:46,765
Next time: if speed is built from
length and time, what is force built

134
00:05:46,765 --> 00:05:47,665
from?

135
00:05:47,306 --> 00:05:50,423
We find out in fundamental
and derived quantities.
